DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       _c_u_r_s_e_s provides applications the ability to write the contents  of  the
       screen  to  a  file and read them back.  To read/write a window (rather
       than the whole screen) from/to a file, use ggeettwwiinn(3X)  and  ppuuttwwiinn(3X),
       respectively.

   ssccrr__dduummpp
       ssccrr__dduummpp  writes  to  _f_i_l_e_n_a_m_e  the contents of the virtual screen; see
       ccuurrssccrr(3X).

   ssccrr__rreessttoorree
       ssccrr__rreessttoorree updates the virtual screen to contain the contents of _f_i_l_e_‐
       _n_a_m_e  (if  it  was  validly written with ssccrr__dduummpp).  No refresh is per‐
       formed; after performing any further desired updates, call ddoouuppddaattee(3X)
       or similar.

   ssccrr__iinniitt
       ssccrr__iinniitt  reads _f_i_l_e_n_a_m_e, using it to initialize _c_u_r_s_e_s data structures
       describing the state of the terminal screen.  If these data are  valid,
       _c_u_r_s_e_s  bases  its next update of the screen on this information rather
       than clearing it and starting from scratch.

       The data fail the validity check

       •   if the terminal employs _t_e_r_m_i_n_f_o capabilities eexxiitt__ccaa__mmooddee  (rrmmccuupp)
           or nnoonn__rreevv__rrmmccuupp (nnrrrrmmcc) are defined, or

       •   if  _c_u_r_s_e_s  knows  that  the terminal has been written to since the
           preceding ssccrr__dduummpp call.

       ssccrr__iinniitt could be used after iinniittssccrr(3X)  or  ssyysstteemm(3)  to  share  the
       screen with another process that has done a ssccrr__dduummpp after eennddwwiinn(3X).

   ssccrr__sseett
       The  ssccrr__sseett  routine is a combination of ssccrr__rreessttoorree and ssccrr__iinniitt.  It
       tells the program that the information in _f_i_l_e_n_a_m_e is what is currently
       on the screen, and also what the program wants on the screen.  This can
       be thought of as a screen inheritance function.

RREETTUURRNN VVAALLUUEE
       These functions return OOKK on success and EERRRR on failure.

       X/Open defines no failure conditions.   In  this  implementation,  each
       function fails if it cannot open _f_i_l_e_n_a_m_e.

